 work, we introduce a novel approach to analyze the asymptotic behavior of solutions to a specific type of Kirchhoff equations, which are defined as

\begin{equation*}
u^{\Delta\Delta} + \Delta W(t,x,s^{\frac{1}{m}})u^{\frac{m+1}{m}} = 0
\end{equation*} 
in the context of a time-like changing variable. The potential function W is assumed to be non-negative and satisfies a specific condition related to its Hessian matrix. We establish the existence of a finite time t_b, at which the solution u becomes extinct, and provide an explicit expression for t_b. Furthermore, we demonstrate that the solution u satisfies a specific decay rate as it approaches extinction. This research builds upon previous studies by Kirchhoff, Ambrosetti, and Rabinowitz, and the results obtained in this work are more comprehensive and applicable to a broader range of scenarios. This research also extends the findings of a previous study by Yang and Wang. This work is part of the research conducted under the auspices of the National Natural Science Foundation of China. This research was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China. This research was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China. This research was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China. This research was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China. This research was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of
